Documentation for adder
-----------------------
rev 0.2, 2/5/99:   Added support for HalfHeight layout
rev 0.3, 2/19/99:  Changed from TSMC25 icons to MMI generic icons
rev 0.4, 3/9/99:   Updated to final MMI generic icons;
                   Partitioned adder now outputs the sub-chain carry out's
rev 0.5, 3/26/99:  UsePrevCout property added, to allow the carry-in to the
                   msb of a chain to be visible outside the adder.
rev 0.6, 4/30/99:  Added netlist_level property to icon, so that it will
                   default to "schematic" (not "icon") in sue's dpc & verilog
                   modes.
                   Fixed bug in hookup of final carry out for partitioned
                   adder.
                   Fixed error checking on Partition to catch null input.
                   Modified cells to speed up carry propagation:
                   cs_startp_n0C_H, cs_startp_n0C_L, cs_startp_n0B_H,
                   cs_startp_n0B_L.  Added MMI_ADDMUX3C.
rev 0.7, 5/10/99:  Changed names of cells to begin with MMIG_ADD_
rev 0.8, 7/16/99:  Added NAME_adder proc, which creates adder's name


The adder generator is used to derive adders of the following types:
  - ripple carry
  - carry select

These are the input arguments:

  NBITS:  
    Definition:  
      The number of bits in the adder.
    Legal Values:  
      The ripple carry adder allows 1 <= NBITS <= 128.
      The carry select adder allows 4 <= NBITS <= 128, since a ripple adder 
      is more efficient than a carry select adder for just a few bits.
    Default:  
      32

  Type:
    Definition:
      The adder algorithm used.
    Legal Values:
      Carry Select ("CS") or Ripple ("Ripple")
    Default:
      Carry Select

  Partition:
    Definition:
      Either type of adder can be partitioned into smaller adders.  When
      you designate a bit as a partition bit, a mux is added to the carry in
      to that bit.  If the "Inject" signal corresponding to that bit position
      is asserted, the value of C_H is used as the carry in to that bit;
      otherwise, the carry out of the previous bit is used.  The carry out
      of each partition is an output of the adder.
    Legal Values:
      No partitioning ("N"), or a list of bit positions separated by spaces, 
      i.e. "1 5 27".  The legal values for the bit positions are 1 to
      (NBITS - 1), inclusive.
    Default:
      N

  UsePrevCout:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the carry-in's to the msb of any chain (meaning
      the msb of any partition, and the msb of the entire adder) to be 
      visible outside the adder.  Useful for overflow/underflow detection.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are "Y" (make carry-in's visible)
      and "N" (don't).
    Default:
      N
      
  OutputSize:
    Definition:
      This argument selects the final drive size of the adder.  
    Legal Values:
      The supported strengths are A, B, and C.
    Default:
      C

  HalfHeight:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify that the layout be done in
      (NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 bit positions, instead of (NBITS_A + NBITS_B) 
      bit positions.  Note that if (NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 is an odd number of
      bit positions, the layout will actually fit in 
      ((NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 + 1) bit positions.
      This configuration results in a slightly slower and less area 
      efficient design.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are "Y" (half height layout)
      and "N" (full height layout).
    Default:
      N

  bitslice:
    Definition:
      This argument directs DPC to enforce bitslice layout, or not.  
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are "Y" and "N".
    Default:
      Y


------------------------------------------------------------------------
A note on the meaning of the underlying cell names:

All cells used in the adder begin with MMIG_ADD_.  The rest of the name
is formed as four fields, as follows:

1.  R  : Ripple Carry
    C  : Carry Select

2.  B  : Base Cell
    Bc : Base + CarryIn driven as cell output
    S  : Chain starter cell
    S0 : Chain starter cell, optimized for bit position 0 only
    Sp : Chain starter cell, used for partition starts
    Sc : Chain starter cell + CarryIn driven as cell output
    F  : Final cell
    Fc : Final cell + CarryIn driven as cell output
    Fp : Final cell, also a partition start

3.  H  : carry input(s) are High asserted
    L  : carry input(s) are Low asserted

4.  A  : output driver size A
    B  : output driver size B
    C  : output driver size C

Thus, the cell MMIG_ADD_RBcHB is:
  - R: used in the ripple carry adder
  - Bc: is a base cell, which also drives its carry in as a cell output
  - H: has high-asserted carry in
  - B: has output drive strength B

